      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "To elucidate the effects of anionic and cationic polyelectrolyte substance on bronchoconstriction, we examined the serial changes in respiratory resistance (Rrs) in ovalbumin-sensitized guinea pigs after antigen exposure with or without pre-inhalation of low-molecular-weight heparin, poly-L-glutamic acid, poly-L-lysine and dextran, and with or without oral intake of dalteparin. Both immediate and late responses after antigen exposure were significantly decreased after pretreatment with inhaled low-molecular-weight heparin and poly-L-glutamic acid compared with saline alone. The late response was significantly decreased after pretreatment with oral dalteparin. Both low-molecular-weight heparin and poly-L-glutamic acid significantly decreased the airway response to methacholine in sensitized guinea pigs. In sensitized guinea pigs, the airway response to methacholine was significantly increased after pretreatment with inhaled poly-L-lysine. Pretreatment with inhaled low-molecular-weight heparin before poly-L-lysine exposure significantly suppressed the airway hyperresponsiveness after inhaled poly-L-lysine. These findings indicated that the \"cationic-anionic interaction\" plays an important role in airway responsiveness."},
